THE KEY RATIO:
  ln(3)/ln(2) = log2(3) = 1.5849625007
  This is the 'Collatz constant' — the up/down ratio.
  If this were rational (p/q), Collatz would be easy to prove.
  But log2(3) is TRANSCENDENTAL (Gelfond-Schneider).

  OUR CONNECTION:
  On the Cayley line: 3 = Q(1/2), so 'multiply by 3' = step to x=1/2.
  And 2 = Q(1/3), so 'divide by 2' = step back to x=1/3.
  The DIFFERENCE in Cayley position: arctanh(1/2) - arctanh(1/3)
    = ln(3)/2 - ln(2)/2 = ln(3/2)/2 = 0.202733 nats
  This is EXACTLY the information content of one Collatz 'up' step
  after the first halving: net gain = ln(3/2)/2 = half the fifth.
  In music: the interval 3:2 = the PERFECT FIFTH.
  The Collatz conjecture is about whether the FIFTH always resolves!

======================================================================
2. THE RIEMANN HYPOTHESIS — TOURNAMENT ZETA APPROXIMATION
======================================================================

Our spectral zeta: ζ_M(s) = τ3^{-s} + λ_c^{-s} + λ̄_c^{-s}
  - 3 terms (one real pole + one complex pair)
  - All zeros on Re(s) = 0
  - ζ_M(-n) = Tr(M^n) = tribonacci trace (integers!)

The Riemann zeta: ζ(s) = Σ n^{-s}
  - ∞ terms (one per natural number)
  - All non-trivial zeros conjecturally on Re(s) = 1/2
  - ζ(-n) = B_{n+1}/(n+1) (Bernoulli numbers, rational!)

QUESTION: Can we BUILD a sequence of tournament zetas that
APPROXIMATES the Riemann zeta in some limit?

IDEA: The k-nacci transfer matrix M_k has k eigenvalues.
As k → ∞, the spectral zeta has k terms.
Does ζ_{M_k}(s) → ζ(s) or η(s) in some sense?

k-nacci spectral zetas at s=2:
